<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1169">1169</a>–<a href="/years/1171">1171</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Norman invasion of Ireland · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland began in 1169, when Norman knights from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> landed to support the deposed king of Leinster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>. Led from 1170 by Richard de Clare, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, and in 1171 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> came to Ireland and received the submission of many Irish kings. It began more than eight centuries of English involvement in Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland.jpg" alt="Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Maclise · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MarriageAoifeStrongbow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Twelfth-century Gaelic Ireland was made up of over-kingdoms under a High King who took tribute but did not rule a unitary state, while the Norse-Irish held the port towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>. Norman lords had already conquered much of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In 1155 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> discussed invading Ireland at a council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, and Anglo-Norman churchmen, wanting control of the Irish church and full Gregorian reform, backed intervention; the papal bull Laudabiliter, or an equivalent, was said to authorise it.</p><p>In 1166 most Irish kings acknowledged Ruaidrí Ua Conchobair of Connacht as High King, and his coalition ousted Diarmait mac Murchada from Leinster. Diarmait sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, swore loyalty to Henry and won leave to recruit Marcher lords, among them Richard de Clare, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, Robert FitzStephen and Maurice FitzGerald, promising Strongbow his daughter Aoífe and the succession to Leinster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In May 1170 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raymond-fitzgerald">Raymond FitzGerald</a> landed at Bannow Bay, routed a larger force at Baginbun and executed 70 captives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> landed at Passage on 23 August with at least 200 knights and 1,000 soldiers, stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, married Aoífe and, crossing the Wicklow Mountains to avoid Ruaidrí's army, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. After Diarmait died in May 1171 most of the Irish kingdoms besieged Dublin for two months, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> held most of their territory.</p><p>Fearing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a> might found an independent kingdom, Henry landed with a large army in October 1171. He granted Strongbow Leinster as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, took the Norse-Irish towns as crown land, called the Synod of Cashel to reform the church and received the submission of many Irish kings, while granting Meath to Hugh de Lacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Fighting went on after Henry left in 1172. Domnall <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrien-dynasty">Ua Briain</a>'s forces defeated part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>'s army at the Battle of Thurles in 1174. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-windsor-1175">Treaty of Windsor</a> of 6 October 1175 made Henry overlord of the conquered land and Ruaidrí of the rest, but it soon collapsed, and in 1177 Henry made his son John Lord of Ireland and allowed further conquest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> brought castles, charters, coinage and new crops and animals. Contemporaries called the invaders English, and historians still differ over whether to call them Normans or English.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Anglo-Norman invasion</b> of 1169, Kilkenny became the seat of English lords, and the Mac Giolla Phádraig held only Upper Ossory.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-osraige">Kingdom of Osraige</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Anglo-Norman invasion</b> the FitzGeralds of Kildare took their eastern lands, but the O'Connors Faly held the rest, raiding the Pale and taking protection payments known as black rent.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ui-failge">Kingdom of Ui Failge</a></li></ul></section>
